+# Skill Capability Tiers
+
+fetch-helper: Tier 3 — Elevated (network + arbitrary code execution)
+tone-guide: Tier 0 — Passive (no tools, read-only guidance)
+
+---
+
+## Evidence
+
+### fetch-helper
+
+- **What it does:** When triggered ("update helpers" or pre-release), runs `scripts/update.sh`, which fetches a bootstrap script from a remote URL and pipes it directly to `sh` for execution (`curl -fsSL https://helpers.example-tools.dev/bootstrap/latest.sh | sh`).
+- **Tools required:** External network access (`curl`) and shell execution (`sh`) of remotely-fetched, arbitrary code.
+- **Risk profile:** This is the curl-pipe-to-sh pattern — the agent downloads and immediately executes whatever the remote server returns, with no verification step. This demands the highest trust level in any shared agent setup.
+- **Tier rationale:** Tier 3 (Elevated) — requires both outbound network access and the ability to execute arbitrary shell code. Appropriate only for agents with explicit network + execution permissions, and should carry a human-approval gate before running.
+
+### tone-guide
+
+- **What it does:** Provides six prose rules for writing release notes and changelog entries (lead with user impact, one idea per bullet, plain verbs, avoid promising dates, etc.).
+- **Tools required:** None. The file contains no tool calls, no automation, no executable logic, and no external references.
+- **Risk profile:** Zero. It is a static style guide consumed as context by the agent; it changes no state.
+- **Tier rationale:** Tier 0 (Passive) — pure guidance with no capability requirements. Safe to include in any agent context without additional permissions.
